 still is from neutral.", "alerts" : "The newest 60 alerts: news = a failure on a scheduled release, correlation = the peers closed the opposite way to the market, friday = the weekly confirmation. Labels only; nothing here places an order. Each alert also carries the readings behind it: z (the market's own day move in 20-day ranges, up = positive), peer_z (the peers' median move, stored positive = the way the set up's crowd was leaning: UP for a short set up, DOWN for a long one; multiply by +1 for a short set up and -1 for a long one to get the peers' move in price terms), the index at alert, entry_note, stop_rule, failure_day, atr, news[] and peers[]. Each news[] item also carries fact (the plain words for that release, e.g. inflation hotter than forecast; empty if none) and read (bullish, bearish, in line or no clear read, for THIS market), ready to print; first_minute.move in it is stored the same crowd-signed way as peer_z. Since 2026-11-27 each news[] item also carries country (whose release: US, Australia ...), release (which one, with its period when the source gave it, e.g. US GDP (Q3, second estimate)), series[] (the released figures: label, period, actual, forecast, previous, vs = which way it came out, e.g. stronger / hotter / as forecast), surprise and why (the one-line reason the news is bullish / bearish for THIS market, ending in that same word; empty when there is no read) and text (the whole line). Alerts stored before then get country, release and why filled here, and figures only when that day's news-watch row holds them.", "news_watch" : "Per market, the scheduled events of the last 7 and next 7 days. Before a release: the forecast as last read, and the news to watch for (crowd_outcome, already in plain bullish / bearish words for the market). After: the news against forecast (news_class WITH / AGAINST is the stored crowd-relative class; read it as bullish / bearish for the market with the set up direction: a short set up WITH = bullish, AGAINST = bearish; a long set up the reverse), the first minute, the close, and a verdict. verdict: failure_full = the news pointed one way, the first minute went that way, the close went the other; failure = the news pointed one way, the close went the other; followed_news / shrugged / no_failure = no failure; against_unread = closed against the set up on a news day with no clear news read; pending = no close yet. strict = the three-step rule; alerted = a news alert was sent that day (the looser live rule). Since 2026-11-27 each entry also carries country, release (with its period) and, for a judged bullish / bearish row, why; series[] items carry label and period. Labels only.", "not_claimed" : "Display only. Nothing here feeds a trade, a grade, an alert or any sizing."}}
